\f1\b\fs36 \cf2 Interpreting Your Leadership Profile\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 Your profile reflects a leader who is likely highly strategic, comfortable with risk, and focused on driving results, but who also holds a genuine commitment to fairness and continuous learning.\
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Duality:
\f0\b0  Your highest risk signal (Psychopathy at 68) suggests a tendency toward rapid, sometimes impulsive action, high risk tolerance, and potential bluntness. However, your strong Humility (75) and Ethical Leadership (68) act as powerful internal safeguards. You might occasionally move too fast or speak too sharply, but you possess the self-awareness to recognize it and the willingness to correct course when you realize you have overstepped.\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Workplace Triggers:
\f0\b0  High-pressure situations, slow-moving consensus building, or bureaucratic roadblocks might trigger a "just get it done" impulse. In these moments, the moderate Machiavellian (64) and elevated Psychopathy signals might combine, tempting you to bypass rules, force progress, or selectively share information.\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Context-Sensitive Risk Points:
\f0\b0  Because your Empathy (61) is still an emerging capacity, you might occasionally misread how your bluntness or rapid operational shifts affect team morale. In high-stress environments, your focus on the objective may inadvertently overshadow the human impact.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\li4620\fi-4620\ri4620\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf2 Top 3 Behavioral Risks to Monitor\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Impulsive Action-Bias:
\f0\b0  A tendency to make rapid decisions or bend procedural rules to achieve a goal quickly, potentially overlooking secondary consequences or team burnout.\uc0\u8232 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Strategic Information Siloing:
\f0\b0  The temptation to over-manage narratives, withhold the full context of a decision, or use information asymmetry to maintain influence and prevent resistance.\uc0\u8232 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Task-Over-Tone (Bluntness):
\f0\b0  Delivering feedback or directives with low emotional sensitivity, prioritizing absolute efficiency over the psychological safety of the recipient.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\li4620\fi-4620\ri4620\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf2 Top 3 Protective Strengths\
